# Byte-pinned artifacts must be byte-STABLE across platforms.
#
# The vendored omega_one trust subset is pinned by a per-file SHA-256 map in
# VENDOR_MANIFEST.json. Git's line-ending normalisation rewrote all ten modules to CRLF on a
# Windows checkout, so every pinned hash mismatched and `test_vendor_manifest_no_drift` failed --
# on Windows only. In Linux CI the same commit was green. The vendored bytes were never wrong;
# the checkout changed them underneath the pin.
#
# A drift detector that fires on the platform rather than on drift is worse than none: it trains
# people to ignore it, and it did -- the failure was written off as pre-existing in a previous
# session's commit message.
#
# `-text` means "never convert this file's line endings in either direction."
src/hazardpulse/trust/_vendor_omega/** -text
src/hazardpulse/trust/_vendor_omega/VENDOR_MANIFEST.json -text

# Forecast receipts and evidence ledgers are hashed and signed. Same reasoning: a signature over
# bytes is meaningless if the checkout is free to rewrite them.
dist/data/evidence/** -text
dist/data/replay/** -text

# Test fixtures pinned by SHA-256 (tests/test_nws_live.py): byte-exact on every platform.
tests/fixtures/nws_live/** -text
